#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383568 :question/problem "Consider a small rectangle $Δ$ whose bottom-left corner is located at the point $(2,1)$ with sides parallel to the coordinate axes in the $uv$-plane and whose area is $\\delta A.$ Which of the following gives the best approximation for the area of the image of this rectangle under the action of the transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{ln}(u-v) \\\\ v-{u}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the absolute value of the Jacobian determinant gives the local area scale factor of the transformation at that point:\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(T(Δ))≈|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\cdot \\underbrace{\\text{Area}(Δ)}_{\\delta A}\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{1}{u-v} & -\\frac{1}{u-v} \\\\ -2u & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{u-v}\\cdot 1-(-2u)\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{u-v}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1-2u}{u-v} \\\\ {\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(2,1)} & ={\\left(\\frac{1-2u}{u-v}\\right)\\,|}_{u=2\\,v=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-2(2)}{2-1} \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(Δ)) & ≈|-3|\\cdot \\delta A \\\\  & =3\\,\\delta A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383575 :question/problem "Consider a small rectangle $Δ$ whose bottom-left corner is located at the point $(-3,3)$ with sides parallel to the coordinate axes in the $uv$-plane and whose area is $\\delta A.$ Which of the following gives the best approximation for the area of the image of this rectangle under the action of the transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}u+{v}^{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}(u+v)\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the absolute value of the Jacobian determinant gives the local area scale factor of the transformation at that point:\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(T(Δ))≈|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\cdot \\underbrace{\\text{Area}(Δ)}_{\\delta A}\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2v \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)\\cdot 2v \\\\  & =(1-2v)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v) \\\\ {\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(-3,3)} & =(1-2v)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)\\,{|}_{u=-3\\,v=3} \\\\  & =(1-2(3))\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-3+3) \\\\  & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(Δ)) & ≈|-5|\\cdot \\delta A \\\\  & =5\\,\\delta A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383607 :question/problem "If $f(x,y)={e}^{2y-x},x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ and $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}t}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/f0/f01b253f560405a8c0f4be8f8797c652fb0ddf774f4db110b8822313b8ac50f3.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with one independent variable $t,$ where $x=x(t)$ and $y=y(t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=-{e}^{2y-x},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2{e}^{2y-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =-{e}^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =2{e}^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $t,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =-{e}^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+2{e}^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t){e}^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383614 :question/problem "Let $f(x,y,z)=z-x-y,$ where $x={e}^{s}+t,$ $y=s+1$ and $z=1-t-s.$ Calculate $\\frac{∂f}{∂s}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/be/be6afcdd0ad9ae0d7ef377c4918442a64e1186c90c25e27ec6c224d816a602fd.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y,z)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t),y=y(s,t)$ and $z=z(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂s}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\cdot \\frac{∂z}{∂s}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x,y$ and $z:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=-1,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-1,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ with respect to $s,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂s} & ={e}^{s}, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂s} & =1, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂s} & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂s} & =(-1)\\cdot {e}^{s}+(-1)\\cdot 1+(1)\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-{e}^{s}-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385331 :question/problem "Given  $g(x,y,z)={e}^{2xz}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2}-1)$, find $∇g(x,y,z).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{x}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{2xz}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2}-1)) \\\\  & =2z{e}^{2xz}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2}-1) \\\\ {g}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{2xz}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2}-1)) \\\\  & =2y{e}^{2xz}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({y}^{2}-1) \\\\ {g}_{z}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}({e}^{2xz}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2}-1)) \\\\  & =2x{e}^{2xz}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the gradient is: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}g(x,y,z) & ={g}_{x}(x,y,z)\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+{g}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}+{g}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =2z{e}^{2xz}\\operatorname{sin}({y}^{2}-1)\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+2y{e}^{2xz}\\operatorname{cos}({y}^{2}-1)\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}+2x{e}^{2xz}\\operatorname{sin}({y}^{2}-1)\\text{ }\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =2{e}^{2xz}\\text{ }(z\\operatorname{sin}({y}^{2}-1)\\mathbf{i}+y\\operatorname{cos}({y}^{2}-1)\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}+x\\operatorname{sin}({y}^{2}-1)\\text{ }\\mathbf{k})\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385344 :question/problem "Given $h(x,y,z)=xyz\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xyz)$, find  $∇h(x,y,z).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}_{x}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xyz\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xyz)) \\\\  & =yz\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xyz)+xyz\\frac{yz}{xyz} \\\\  & =yz(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xyz)+1) \\\\ {h}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xyz\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xyz)) \\\\  & =xz\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xyz)+xyz\\frac{xz}{xyz} \\\\  & =xz(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xyz)+1) \\\\ {h}_{z}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(xyz\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xyz)) \\\\  & =xy\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xyz)+xyz\\frac{xy}{xyz} \\\\  & =xy(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xyz)+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the gradient is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}h(x,y,z) & ={h}_{x}(x,y,z)\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+{h}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}+{h}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =yz(\\operatorname{ln}(xyz)+1)\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+xz(\\operatorname{ln}(xyz)+1)\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}+xy(\\operatorname{ln}(xyz)+1)\\text{ }\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}(xyz)+1)(yz\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+xz\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}+xy\\text{ }\\mathbf{k}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385363 :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-2{y}^{2},$ where $x=s\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$ and $y=t\\operatorname{sin}⁡s.$ Calculate $\\frac{∂f}{∂s}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/21/21cec766fe72965697304ef4288ffb2b090ddfca179210165dbcfd42ec490b27.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t)$ and $y=y(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂s}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂s}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2x,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-4y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s$ and $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =2s\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =-4t\\operatorname{sin}⁡s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $s,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂s} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂s} & =t\\operatorname{cos}⁡s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂s} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂s} \\\\  & =(2s\\operatorname{sin}t)(\\operatorname{sin}t)+(-4t\\operatorname{sin}s)(t\\operatorname{cos}s) \\\\  & =2s{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t-4{t}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}s\\operatorname{cos}s \\\\  & =2s{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t-2{t}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}2s.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385370 :question/problem "Let $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+{y}^{2}z,$ where $x=3t,$ $y=t-s$ and $z=2st.$ Given that\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂t}=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t+{c}_{1}s{t}^{2}+{c}_{2}{s}^{2}t+{c}_{3}{s}^{3},\n$$\n\nfind the value of ${c}_{1}+{c}_{2}+{c}_{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/b5/b593a1b60fe8c1aef2c5c229ff426db350e6296b9ecb767494283f0fa3342072.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y,z)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t),y=y(s,t)$ and $z=z(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂t}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\cdot \\frac{∂z}{∂t}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x,y$ and $z:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2yz,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}={y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s$ and $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =2(t-s)(2st)=4s{t}^{2}-4{s}^{2}t, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =(t-s)^{2}={t}^{2}-2st+{s}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ with respect to $t,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂t} & =3, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂t} & =1, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂t} & =2s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂t} & =(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t)\\cdot 3+(4s{t}^{2}-4{s}^{2}t)\\cdot 1+({t}^{2}-2st+{s}^{2})\\cdot 2s \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t+4s{t}^{2}-4{s}^{2}t+2s{t}^{2}-4{s}^{2}t+2{s}^{3} \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t+6s{t}^{2}-8{s}^{2}t+2{s}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, ${c}_{1}=6,$ ${c}_{2}=-8$ and ${c}_{3}=2,$ and therefore\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}+{c}_{3}=6+(-8)+2=0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385377 :question/problem "Find the equation of the normal line to the surface $\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}+y-4z}=1$ at the point $(2,1,2).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's set $f(x,y,z)=\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}+y-4z}.$ Then, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=1.$     \n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $(2,1,2)$ can be found by computing $∇f(2,1,2).$\n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(2,1,2):$    \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}+y-4z})\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}+y-4z})\\text{ }j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}+y-4z})\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}+y-4z}}\\text{ }i+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}+y-4z}}\\text{ }j-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}+y-4z}}\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(2,1,2) & =\\frac{2\\cdot 2}{\\sqrt{2\\cdot {2}^{2}+1-4\\cdot 2}}\\text{ }i+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2\\cdot {2}^{2}+1-4\\cdot 2}}\\text{ }j-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2\\cdot {2}^{2}+1-4\\cdot 2}}\\text{ }k \\\\  & =4\\text{ }i+\\frac{1}{2}\\text{ }j-2\\text{ }k \\\\  & =⟨4,\\,\\frac{1}{2},\\,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by using the symmetric form of the equation of the line that is parallel to the vector $\\mathbf{v}=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y},{v}_{z}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$,  we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}}=\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}}=\\frac{z-{z}_{0}}{{v}_{z}} \\\\ \\frac{x-2}{4}=\\frac{y-1}{\\frac{1}{2}}=\\frac{z-2}{-2} \\\\ \\frac{x-2}{4}=2y-2=\\frac{2-z}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385408 :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ defined as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}v\\operatorname{tan}\\left(\\frac{u}{2}\\right) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}v\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T},$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $$\n\\displaystyle x(u,v)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{u}{2}\\right)\n$$ and $y(u,v)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡v.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡v}{2{cos}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{u}{2}\\right)} & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{u}{2}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡v}{2{cos}^{2}⁡(u/2)} & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{u}{2}\\right) \\\\ \\,0\\, & \\,\\text{ }\\text{ }\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡v}{2{cos}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{u}{2}\\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385415 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}=⟨{e}^{xz}\\operatorname{cos}y,\\hspace{0.167em}0,\\hspace{0.167em}\\operatorname{ln}yz⟩.$ Find $\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}.$" :question/worked-solution "If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+R\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}$ is a vector field on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $\\mathbf{F}$ all exist, then the divergence of $\\mathbf{F}$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}=\\mathrm{∇}\\cdot \\mathbf{F}=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}\\cdot \\mathbf{F} & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{xz}\\operatorname{cos}y)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(0)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{ln}yz) \\\\  & =z{e}^{xz}\\operatorname{cos}y+\\frac{1}{z}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385487 :question/problem "A surface $S$ is defined by $z=f(x,y).$ The curve $C$ is the intersection of $S$ with the plane $y=2.$ Given that the point $P$ with $x$-coordinate $3$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=\\frac{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}}}{y-x}$, find the slope of the tangent to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=2.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point where $x=3$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(3,2).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ at $(3,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(3,2) & =\\frac{\\sqrt{(3)^{2}-2(2)^{2}}}{2-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{9-8}}{-1} \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385494 :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Given that the point $P(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},0,2)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y}{2}$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},0).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},0) & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡0}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-1)\\cdot 1}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},0) & ={f}_{y}(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},0)(y-0) \\\\ z-2 & =-\\frac{1}{2}y \\\\ z & =-\\frac{1}{2}y+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},0,2)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\ z &=-\\frac{1}{2}y+2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ a normal vector to the plane is ${\\mathbf{n}}_{1}=⟨1,0,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=-\\frac{1}{2}y+2$ (or $y+2z-4=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${\\mathbf{n}}_{2}=⟨0,1,2⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,0,0⟩\\times ⟨0,1,2⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-2j+k \\\\  & =⟨0,-2,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to $C$ at $P$ is $⟨0,-2,1⟩.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385501 :question/problem "Given  $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)+{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y$, find $∇f(x,y).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)+{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) \\\\  & =y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)+2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)+{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)+{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{y} \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the gradient is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\text{ }\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =(y\\operatorname{cos}(xy)+2x\\operatorname{ln}y)\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+(x\\operatorname{cos}(xy)+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y})\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385508 :question/problem "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}$ that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(5,4).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(5,4)$, can be found by computing $∇f(5,4).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient of $f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(5,4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})\\text{ }j \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\text{ }i-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\text{ }j \\\\ ∇f(5,4) & =\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{(5)^{2}-(4)^{2}}}\\text{ }i-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{(5)^{2}-(4)^{2}}}\\text{ }j \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{9}}i-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{9}}j \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}i-\\frac{4}{3}j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize $∇f(5,4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\mathrm{∇}f(5,4)}{‖\\mathrm{∇}f(5,4)‖} & =\\frac{\\frac{5}{3}\\mathbf{i}-\\frac{4}{3}\\mathbf{j}}{\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{5}{3}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(-\\frac{4}{3}\\right)}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{5}{3}\\mathbf{i}-\\frac{4}{3}\\mathbf{j}}{\\frac{\\sqrt{41}}{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{41}}{41}(5\\mathbf{i}-4\\mathbf{j})\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385515 :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+{y}^{2}=1.$  Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(3,0).$" :question/worked-solution "If we set $f(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+{y}^{2}$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=1.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(3,0)$ can be found by computing $∇f(3,0).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(3,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+{y}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+{y}^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}x\\,i+2y\\,j \\\\ ∇f(3,0) & =\\frac{2}{9}(3)\\,i+2(0)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\,i \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{2}{3},0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(3,0)=⟨\\frac{2}{3},0⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(3,0).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $\\mathbf{v}=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-{x}_{0}){v}_{y}-(y-{y}_{0}){v}_{x} & =0 \\\\ (x-3)0-(y-0)\\frac{2}{3} & =0 \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3}y & =0 \\\\ y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385522 :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)={x}^{2}+y.$  Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(-1,0).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy) & ={x}^{2}+y \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-{x}^{2}-y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-{x}^{2}-y$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(-1,0)$ can be found by computing $∇f(-1,0).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(-1,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-{x}^{2}-y)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-{x}^{2}-y)\\,j \\\\  & =(-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)-2x)\\,i+(-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)-1)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(-1,0) & =(-0\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡((-1)\\cdot 0)-2\\cdot (-1))\\,i+(-(-1)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡((-1)\\cdot 0)-1)\\,j \\\\  & =2\\,i-j \\\\  & =⟨2,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(-1,0)=⟨2,-1⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(-1,0).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $\\mathbf{v}=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}} & =\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}} \\\\ \\frac{x-(-1)}{2} & =\\frac{y-0}{-1} \\\\ \\frac{x+1}{2} & =-y \\\\ x+1 & =-2y \\\\ x+2y+1 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385529 :question/problem "Evaluate $∇h(x,y,z)$ at the point $(1,-1,\\pi )$ if $h(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xyz).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}_{x}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xyz)) \\\\  & =yz\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xyz) \\\\ {h}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xyz)) \\\\  & =xz\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xyz) \\\\ {h}_{z}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xyz)) \\\\  & =xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xyz)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nHence, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇h(x,y,z) & ={h}_{x}(x,y,z)\\text{ }i+{h}_{x}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{h}_{x}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =yz\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xyz)\\text{ }i+xz\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xyz)\\text{ }j+xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xyz)\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇h(1,-1,\\pi ) & =(-1)(\\pi )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-\\pi )\\text{ }i+(1)(\\pi )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-\\pi )\\text{ }j+(1)(-1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-\\pi )\\text{ }k \\\\  & =(-\\pi )(-1)\\text{ }i+\\pi (-1)\\text{ }j-(-1)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\pi \\text{ }i-\\pi \\text{ }j+\\text{ }k\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385536 :question/problem "Given that $g(x,y)=4xy+{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}$, find the points $(x,y)$ where $\\mathrm{∇}g(x,y)=\\mathbf{0}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4xy+{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =4y+0-2x \\\\  & =2(2y-x) \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4xy+{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =4x+2y-0 \\\\  & =2(2x+y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $\\mathrm{∇}g(x,y)=2(2y-x)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2(2x+y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.$\n\nNow, we solve $\\mathrm{∇}g(x,y)=\\mathbf{0}:$\n\n$$\n2(2y-x)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2(2x+y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}=0\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+0\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\nSo, by equating the coefficients of $\\mathbf{i}$ and $\\mathbf{j},$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2y-x &=0 \\\\ 2x+y &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get $x=2y.$ Substituting these into the second equation, we obtain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(2y)+y & =0 \\\\ 5y & =0 \\\\ y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get $x=2\\cdot 0=0.$\n\nTherefore, $\\mathrm{∇}g(x,y)=\\mathbf{0}$ at the point $(0,0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385543 :question/problem "Given that $g(x,y,z)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+y\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+z\\operatorname{ln}⁡z-x-y-z$, find the points $(x,y,z)$ where $\\mathrm{∇}g(x,y,z)=\\mathbf{0}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+y\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+z\\operatorname{ln}⁡z-x-y-z) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+x\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}+0+0-1-0-0 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1-1 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+y\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+z\\operatorname{ln}⁡z-x-y-z) \\\\  & =0+\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+y\\cdot \\frac{1}{y}+0-0-1-0 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+1-1 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡y \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+y\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+z\\operatorname{ln}⁡z-x-y-z) \\\\  & =0+0+\\operatorname{ln}⁡z+z\\cdot \\frac{1}{z}-0-0-1 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡z+1-1 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $\\mathrm{∇}g(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\operatorname{ln}y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\operatorname{ln}z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.$ \n\nNow, we solve $\\mathrm{∇}g(x,y,z)=\\mathbf{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\operatorname{ln}y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\operatorname{ln}z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} & =0\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+0\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}+0\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by equating the coefficient of $\\mathbf{i}$, $\\mathbf{j}$, and $\\mathbf{k}$, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x &=0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y &=0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡z &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get $x=1.$  Similarly, from the second and third equations, we obtain $y=1$ and $z=1.$\n\nHence, $\\mathrm{∇}g(x,y,z)=\\mathbf{0}$ at the point $(1,1,1).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385550 :question/problem "Find the gradient of the product $g(x,y)h(x,y),$ given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(x,y) & ={e}^{xy},\\hspace{2em} &  & \\mathrm{∇}g(x,y)=y{e}^{xy}\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+x{e}^{xy}\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}, &  &  \\\\ h(x,y) & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2},\\hspace{2em} &  & \\mathrm{∇}h(x,y)=2x\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+2y\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}. &  & \\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the product of two functions $g(x,y)$ and $h(x,y)$ is given by:\n\n$$\n∇[gh]=h\\,∇\\,g+g\\,∇\\,h\n$$\n\nUsing the product rule, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}[g(x,y)h(x,y)] & =h(x,y)\\mathrm{∇}g(x,y)+g(x,y)\\mathrm{∇}h(x,y) \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})(y{e}^{xy}\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+x{e}^{xy}\\text{ }\\mathbf{j})+{e}^{xy}(2x\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+2y\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =(({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})y{e}^{xy}\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})x{e}^{xy}\\text{ }\\mathbf{j})+(2x{e}^{xy}\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+2y{e}^{xy}\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =({x}^{2}y+{y}^{3}+2x){e}^{xy}\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+({x}^{3}+x{y}^{2}+2y){e}^{xy}\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385755 :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ defined as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{u}^{2}-{v}^{2} \\\\ {u}^{2}+{v}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T},$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)={u}^{2}-{v}^{2}$ and $y(u,v)={u}^{2}+{v}^{2}.$  \n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =2u & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =-2v \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =2u & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =2v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2u & -2v \\\\ 2u & 2v\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2u\\cdot 2v-(-2v)\\cdot 2u \\\\  & =4uv+4uv \\\\  & =8uv.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385824 :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=y{x}^{2},$ where $x=ts$ and $y=s-t.$ Calculate $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/11/118759b163a4410f66d5855d7b7b1fd9c2ee4be585e778a759997036bd95f765.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t)$ and $y=y(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂t}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂t}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2xy,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}={x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s$ and $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =2(ts)(s-t)=2t{s}^{2}-2s{t}^{2}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =(ts)^{2}={t}^{2}{s}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $t,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂t} & =s, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂t} & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂t} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂t} \\\\  & =(2t{s}^{2}-2s{t}^{2})(s)+({t}^{2}{s}^{2})(-1) \\\\  & =2t{s}^{3}-2{s}^{2}{t}^{2}-{t}^{2}{s}^{2} \\\\  & =2t{s}^{3}-3{t}^{2}{s}^{2} \\\\  & =t{s}^{2}(2s-3t).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385838 :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=y{e}^{x},$ $x=\\sqrt{s}$ and $y=s,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}s}$ for $s>0.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/7e/7e5b5001794ecdf2c9b9e9102f334a63d2c59bc6f7511c2eb7fa358fc94cb0c5.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with one independent variable $s,$ where $x=x(s)$ and $y=y(s),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}s}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}s}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=y{e}^{x},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}={e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =s{e}^{\\sqrt{s}}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & ={e}^{\\sqrt{s}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $s,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}s}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{s}},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}s}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}s} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}s} \\\\  & =s{e}^{\\sqrt{s}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{s}}+{e}^{\\sqrt{s}}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{s}}{2}{e}^{\\sqrt{s}}+{e}^{\\sqrt{s}} \\\\  & =(1+\\frac{\\sqrt{s}}{2}){e}^{\\sqrt{s}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385913 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}=xy{e}^{z}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+yz{e}^{x}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.$ Evaluate $\\mathrm{∇}\\times \\mathbf{F}$ at the point $(0,2,1).$" :question/worked-solution "If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+R\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}$ is a vector field on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{3},$ and the first partial derivatives exist, then the curl of $\\mathbf{F}$ is the vector field on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{3}$ defined by\n\n$$\ncurl\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}=\\mathrm{∇}\\times \\mathbf{F}=\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x} & \\frac{∂}{∂y} & \\frac{∂}{∂z} \\\\ P & Q & R\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x} & \\frac{∂}{∂y} & \\frac{∂}{∂z} \\\\ 0 & xy{e}^{z} & yz{e}^{x}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =[\\frac{∂}{∂y}(yz{e}^{x})-\\frac{∂}{∂z}(xy{e}^{z})]\\mathbf{i}-[\\frac{∂}{∂x}(yz{e}^{x})-\\frac{∂}{∂z}(0)]\\mathbf{j}+[\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy{e}^{z})-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(0)]\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(z{e}^{x}-xy{e}^{z})\\mathbf{i}-(yz{e}^{x}-0)\\mathbf{j}+(y{e}^{z}-0)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(z{e}^{x}-xy{e}^{z})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-yz{e}^{x}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+y{e}^{z}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}$ at the point $(0,2,1),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F})(0,2,1) & =(1\\cdot {e}^{0}-0\\cdot 2\\cdot {e}^{1})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-2\\cdot 1\\cdot {e}^{0}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+2\\cdot {e}^{1}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{i}-2\\mathbf{j}+2e\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386390 :question/problem "Consider the closed curve $C,$ which is the boundary of the region enclosed by the unit circle and the coordinate axes in the first quadrant. Express $$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r},\n$$  where $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=⟨y\\operatorname{cos}x,x-{e}^{-{y}^{2}}⟩,$ as a repeated integral." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n \n\n![](images/e4/e48034aed1f41cf1f9b30c057186879233fe05cd2ba720dcee436398a732b6ce.png)\n\nFirst, notice that we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}={∮}_{C}y\\operatorname{cos}x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}x+(x-{e}^{-{y}^{2}})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y\n$$\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and $Q(x,y)=x-{e}^{-{y}^{2}}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):0\\le x\\le 1,\\hspace{0.167em}0\\le y\\le \\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,dx+(x-{e}^{-{y}^{2}})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386397 :question/problem "Consider the triangle $C$ in the plane with vertices $(0,-1),(1,0)$ and $(0,2),$ and the region $D$ bounded by $C.$ Express $$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r},\n$$  where $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=⟨{y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}x,xy-2y\\operatorname{cos}x⟩,$ as a repeated integral." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/c2/c2e08c233cb31124234ab6e5179998a3bdc148024632c7770bdea46cd37dff83.png)\n\nFirst, notice that we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}={∮}_{C}{y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}x+(xy-2y\\operatorname{cos}x)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y\n$$\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $Q(x,y)=xy-2y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=y+2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):0\\le x\\le 1,\\,x-1\\le y\\le -2x+2\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}{y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,dx+(xy-2y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(y+2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{x-1}^{-2x+2}y\\text{ }dy\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386480 :question/problem "Find the fundamental vector product of the surface generated by the vector-valued function $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)=⟨u,\\hspace{0.222em}u\\operatorname{sin}v,\\hspace{0.222em}v\\operatorname{cos}u⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v),$ the fundamental vector product is defined as ${\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}.$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u\\operatorname{sin}v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v\\operatorname{cos}u)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}\\operatorname{sin}v,\\hspace{0.222em}-v\\operatorname{sin}u⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u\\operatorname{sin}v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v\\operatorname{cos}u)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}u\\operatorname{cos}v,\\hspace{0.222em}\\operatorname{cos}u⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 1 & \\operatorname{sin}v & -v\\operatorname{sin}u \\\\ 0 & u\\operatorname{cos}v & \\operatorname{cos}u\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}v & -v\\operatorname{sin}u \\\\ u\\operatorname{cos}v & \\operatorname{cos}u\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -v\\operatorname{sin}u \\\\ 0 & \\operatorname{cos}u\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & \\operatorname{sin}v \\\\ 0 & u\\operatorname{cos}v\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}u\\operatorname{sin}v+uv\\operatorname{sin}u\\operatorname{cos}v,\\hspace{0.222em}-\\operatorname{cos}u,\\hspace{0.222em}u\\operatorname{cos}v⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387215 :question/problem "![](images/62/62f33dab4305a41a0bc180231fef7015361dec4aac63f14c31fd48a39b8fcd68.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}4x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where $C$ is the section of the curve $x=\\frac{2}{y}$ traversed between the points $(2,1)$ and $(\\frac{1}{3},6),$ as shown above?\n\n*Hint: Use the parameter $y=t.$*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $x=x(y).$ Therefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=x(t)=\\frac{2}{t}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨\\frac{2}{t},\\hspace{0.222em}t⟩,\\hspace{2em}1\\le t\\le 6\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $1\\le t\\le 6$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ and $‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{2}{t}\\right)\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{{t}^{2}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j} \\\\ ‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{\\left(-\\frac{2}{{t}^{2}}\\right)}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{4}{{t}^{4}}+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{4+{t}^{4}}{{t}^{4}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+4}}{{t}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)=4x{y}^{2},$ then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =4\\left(\\frac{2}{t}\\right)(t)^{2} \\\\  & =8t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}4x{y}^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{1}^{6}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{6}8t\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+4}}{{t}^{2}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8{\\int }_{1}^{6}\\frac{\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+4}}{t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387222 :question/problem "Determine which of the following shaded regions depicts the domain of the vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}{x-y}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The function $\\mathbf{F}$ can be expressed in terms of its components $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=P(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}},\\,Q(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}{x-y}.\n$$\n\nWe first find the domain of each component function. Then, we compute their intersection to find the domain of $\\mathbf{F}:$\n\n- The function $P(x,y)$ is defined when the expression under the radical is positive. So, to find the domain, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n3-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}>0\\,⟹\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<3.\n$$\n\nThis defines the region ${D}_{1},$ which is the interior of a circle of radius $\\sqrt{3},$ as shown below:\n\n![](images/f9/f9c3a22a56839165bfd6c3b05f78ca25053553532063192836d0332d2b070436.png)\n\n- The function $Q(x,y)$ is defined everywhere other than when the denominator vanishes. To find where the denominator vanishes, we set it equal to zero and solve:\n\n$$\nx-y=0\\,⟹\\,x=y.\n$$\n\nThis defines the region ${D}_{1},$ which is the entire plane ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2}$ with the line $y=x$ removed:\n\n![](images/a1/a1ef93cc90bdd59b922723c2c2aa2bd670b43e416923892c9db53b36ac6b7e57.png)\n\nFinally, the domain $D$ of $\\mathbf{F}$ is given by ${D}_{1}\\cap {D}_{2},$ shown below.\n\n![](images/f7/f70cab5ec58e63e1751faa65b575a20da748b3e8ee90cffac8f1f3b029bbbd79.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387256 :question/problem "Find the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x-2y).$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $∇f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x-2y)) \\\\  & ={sec}^{2}⁡(x-2y) \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x-2y)) \\\\  & =-2{sec}^{2}⁡(x-2y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & ={\\operatorname{sec}}^{2}(x-2y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-2{\\operatorname{sec}}^{2}(x-2y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387263 :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams represents the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y)=x+{y}^{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $∇f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =1 \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x+{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have to sketch the vector field at the points $(-4,1),$ $(1,1),$ and $(3,-1).$\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the expression for $∇f.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(-4,1) & =i+2\\cdot 1\\,j=i+2\\,j \\\\ ∇f(1,1) & =i+2\\cdot 1\\,j=i+2\\,j \\\\ ∇f(3,-1) & =i+2\\cdot (-1)\\,j=i-2\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we draw the vectors starting at the corresponding points and obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](images/14/1461b0737bf83fee8e16c1e5e102c36c2042e46935550ebc08a0d5774a561cd3.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387270 :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams represents the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y)=x{y}^{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $∇f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2}) \\\\  & ={y}^{2} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =2xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & ={y}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2xy\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have to sketch the vector field at the points $(-2,1),$ $(2,1),$ and $(-2,-1).$\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the expression for $∇f.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(-2,1) & ={1}^{2}\\,i+2\\cdot (-2)\\cdot 1\\,j=i-4j \\\\ ∇f(2,1) & ={1}^{2}\\,i+2\\cdot 2\\cdot 1\\,j=i+4\\,j \\\\ ∇f(-2,-1) & =(-1)^{2}\\,i+2\\cdot (-2)\\cdot (-1)\\,j=i+4\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we draw the vectors starting at the corresponding points and obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](images/02/020c3ec7cd0e5e57bb4c6de8516988187c9038d9e5598b116966b71fa8b917d8.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387277 :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams represents the vector field $$\n\\displaystyle F(x,y)=\\frac{y}{xy+1}\\,i+\\frac{x}{xy+1}\\,j\n$$ at the indicated points?" :question/worked-solution "We have to sketch the vector field at the points $(-2,0),(1,-2),$ and $(0,2).$\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the function $$\n\\displaystyle F(x,y)=\\frac{y}{xy+1}\\,i+\\frac{x}{xy+1}\\,j\\,:\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(-2,0) & =\\frac{0}{(-2)(0)+1}\\,i+\\frac{-2}{(-2)(0)+1}\\,j=-2\\,j \\\\ F(1,-2) & =\\frac{-2}{(1)(-2)+1}\\,i+\\frac{1}{(1)(-2)+1}\\,j=2\\,i-j \\\\ F(0,2) & =\\frac{2}{(0)(2)+1}\\,i+\\frac{0}{(0)(2)+1}\\,j=2\\,i\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now draw our vectors. At each point, we draw the vector associated with that point, placing the tail end of the vector on the point itself. This gives the following diagram.\n\n![](images/8e/8ec9af02a374c1b8a954117ad055f78e9e2ac74bc391bb230968198a3a474fef.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387284 :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams represents the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=(y-2x)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(2y-4x)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ at the indicated points?" :question/worked-solution "We have to sketch the vector field at the points $(1,3),(-1,-1),$ and $(3,2).$\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the function $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=(y-2x)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(2y-4x)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\\hspace{0.167em}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(1,3) & =(3-2(1))\\,i+(2(3)-4(1))\\,j=i+2\\,j \\\\ F(-1,-1) & =(-1-2(-1))\\,i+(2(-1)-4(-1))\\,j=i+2\\,j \\\\ F(3,2) & =(2-2(3))\\,i+(2(2)-4(3))\\,j=-4\\,i-8\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now draw our vectors. At each point, we draw the vector associated with that point, placing the tail end of the vector on the point itself. This gives the following diagram.\n\n![](images/d6/d688cdc804635688df6780bfb674622a491528c85802397cc3dccb6bd4a2d6be.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387291 :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams represents the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=(y-2)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(x+2)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ at the indicated points?" :question/worked-solution "We have to sketch the vector field at the points $(-5,3),(0,0),$ and $(2,-3).$\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the function $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=(y-2)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(x+2)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\\hspace{0.167em}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(-5,3) & =(3-2)\\,i+(-5+2)\\,j=i-3\\,j \\\\ F(0,0) & =(0-2)\\,i+(0+2)\\,j=-2\\,i+2\\,j \\\\ F(2,-3) & =(-3-2)\\,i+(2+2)\\,j=-5\\,i+4\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now draw our vectors. At each point, we draw the vector associated with that point, placing the tail end of the vector on the point itself. This gives the following diagram.\n\n![](images/d6/d6084f0711f728a09a78c9803bec9c6e54274d6d72a2adc2b1547651e0ec702c.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387304 :question/problem "![](images/50/50bd80ab9aa2f8d5ac2a453ccfe63815a75379efa7502cf1090eb015264206f6.png)\n\nThe gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y)$ is shown above. Which of the following could be a contour plot of $f(x,y)?$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient field vectors of a function $f(x,y)$ always lie perpendicular to its level curves.\n\nObserve that the given field vectors are perpendicular to the level curves in the following picture.\n\n![](images/b7/b73b05cb7f58bcce1d684c7f19c391ecd2033eb4c47c59daf896546956a9d42f.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\n![](images/b8/b8b4cae5128667b4a75097540bddc8dbb853744aee65e96204cf630ccb30fc68.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387311 :question/problem "![](images/f7/f7dfad4e44184f68de36d455e99895fce8c879d4b053c06aa5805c423bdb1bfc.png)\n\nThe gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y)$ is shown above. Which of the following could be a contour plot of $f(x,y)?$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient field vectors of a function $f(x,y)$ always lie perpendicular to its level curves.\n\nObserve that the given field vectors are perpendicular to the level curves in the following picture.\n\n![](images/9f/9f22df07aac0933106381dce4571e674d656428bf7a976e16bed6d46898f814f.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\n![](images/ce/cecc7f28c20523aac9f153022d64da4233ce995e05df3b3e2886b7fb3b20d089.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387367 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral of the function $f(x,y)={e}^{x+y},$ traversed along the line segment from $(0,0)$ to $(-3,4)?$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(0,0)$ to $(-3,4).$ \n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $\\mathbf{a}=\\mathbf{0}$ and $\\mathbf{b}=-3\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+4\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r}(t) & =\\mathbf{a}+t\\hspace{0.167em}(\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a}) \\\\  & =\\mathbf{0}+t\\hspace{0.167em}(\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{0}) \\\\  & =t(\\mathbf{b}) \\\\  & =t(-3\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+4\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =-3t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+4t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=-3t$ and $y=4t$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ and $‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-3t)\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4t)\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =-3\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+4\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\ ‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25} \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)={e}^{x+y}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & ={e}^{-3t+4t} \\\\  & ={e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{t}\\cdot 5\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =5{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387381 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral of the function $f(x,y,z)=\\sqrt{2x+y-z},$ traversed along the line segment from $(3,-2,3)$ to $(5,-1,7)?$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(3,-2,3)$ to $(5,-1,7).$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $\\mathbf{a}=3\\mathbf{i}-2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+3\\mathbf{k}$ and $\\mathbf{b}=5\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}+7\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r}(t) & =\\mathbf{a}+t\\hspace{0.167em}(\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a}) \\\\  & =(3\\mathbf{i}-2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+3\\mathbf{k})+t[(5\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}+7\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k})-(3\\mathbf{i}-2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+3\\mathbf{k})] \\\\  & =(3\\mathbf{i}-2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+3\\mathbf{k})+t(2\\mathbf{i}+\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+4\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =(2t+3)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(t-2)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+(4t+3)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=2t+3,y=t-2,$ and $z=4t+3$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ and $‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t+3)\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t-2)\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4t+3)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =2\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+4\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\ ‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{21}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y,z)=\\sqrt{2x+y-z}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =\\sqrt{2(2t+3)+(t-2)-(4t+3)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{t+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{t+1}\\cdot \\sqrt{21}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{21}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{t+1}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
